# A NATURAL TRANSFORMATION BETWEEN MODELS — the declaration that was
# missing.
#
# A theory has models, and two models of one theory are two functors out
# of the presented category; a map between them is a NATURAL
# TRANSFORMATION.  `transformation Len : ListMonoid ⇒ IntSum = len` says
# that `len` is a homomorphism: for every slot `s : Σ ⇒ Θ` of the
# theory, the square
#
#     ListMonoid@s ; Len(Θ)  =  Len(Σ) ; IntSum@s
#
# commutes, where the component at a stack is the component ON EACH
# WIRE that is the theory's parameter — monoidal, so at `a a` it is
# `len len`.  Because the base is the FREE category on the theory's
# generators, one square per generator is complete: a morphism of
# presentations is determined on generators, so there is nothing else
# to check.
#
# The elaborator generates the squares and DECIDES them: `sameCode`
# where the normalizer reaches, and where it does not, the theory's own
# evidence — `sample : • ⇒ a` supplies the inputs, an exit observes a
# carrier, `eq?` compares.  A square that neither proves nor samples is
# refused, naming the slot and asking for a `sample` slot.

theory Monoid(a) =
    unit   : • ⇒ a
    op     : a a ⇒ a
    sample : • ⇒ a
    law leftUnit = (sample ; unit ... ; op) sample ; eq? ; (forget ; true | forget ; false) ; merge
    law assoc = (sample sample sample ; op _ ; op) (sample sample sample ; _ op ; op) ; eq? ; (forget ; true | forget ; false) ; merge

model IntSum in Monoid(Int) =
    unit   = 0
    op     = +
    sample = 7

# The samples must correspond too — `sample` is a generator like any
# other, and a homomorphism preserves it.  A seven-element list is what
# `len` sends to IntSum's 7, and stating it that way is the point: the
# evidence a model is audited with is part of what a transformation must
# respect.
model ListMonoid in Monoid(List(Int)) =
    unit   = nil
    op     = append
    sample = 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 ; pack

def len = [(acc x -> acc ; _ 1 ; +)] 0 ... ; fold

transformation Len in ListMonoid ⇒ IntSum = len
#   Len : List(a) ⇒ Int — the component is an ordinary word, so it is
#   usable in base code under its own name.  It is FORWARD-DECLARED at
#   `List(Int) ⇒ Int`, the type the two model heads wrote, so a def may
#   name it wherever the declaration sits; its own type is `len`'s, and
#   `len` counts anything.

1 2 3 ; pack ; Len ; print                     # 3

# None of these three squares is PROVED, and the refusals say why in
# one word each: `nil ; len` and `append ; len` stop at `ev` of a value
# whose arrow is not closed (a fold APPLIES its handler, and the
# handler's arrow is open), and the sample square stops at `pack`,
# which has no closed arity.  All three are decided at the theory's
# samples instead — which is exactly what the `sample` slot is for, and
# why a theory without one gets a refusal naming the slot it could not
# decide.  `:transformations` prints the reason beside the verdict:
#
#     Len : ListMonoid ⇒ IntSum
#       unit    sampled (ev of an open wire)
#       op      sampled (2 points; ev of an open wire)
#       sample  sampled (`pack` has no closed arity)

# ---- the same declaration over a DOCTRINE --------------------------
# When the two models are models of a theory that extends `Doctrine`,
# they are internal categories, and a transformation between them is an
# INTERNAL FUNCTOR: the squares for `compose` and `embed` are exactly
# functoriality — preserves composition, preserves the image of the
# base.  There is no strength square, because since 2026-09-16 there is
# no strength: a hom-object names a whole STACK on each side, so a wide
# stage embeds as itself.
#
# Here is one worth having: a function that CARRIES ITS NAME, and the
# functor that forgets the name.

data Named(a..., b...) = Fn⟨a ⇒ b⟩ Str
data Plain(a..., b...) = Fn⟨a ⇒ b⟩

theory Arrow(k(..., ...)) in Doctrine =
    embed   : Fn⟨a ⇒ b⟩ ⇒ k(a, b)
    compose : k(a, b) k(b, c) ⇒ k(a, c)
    observe : k(Int, Int) ⇒ Int
    sample  : • ⇒ k(Int, Int)

# the named category: composition concatenates the names
def embedN   = (f -> f "" ; Named)
def composeN = (c d -> c ; unNamed ; (f s -> d ; unNamed ; (g t -> [f ... ; ev ; g ... ; ev] (s t ; cat) ; Named)))
def witnessN = (c -> c ; unNamed ; _ drop)
def runN     = witnessN ... ; ev
def observeN = (c -> c 3 ; runN ; (y -> c 4 ; runN ; (z -> y z ; +)))

model Names in Arrow(Named) =
    embed   = embedN
    compose = composeN
    observe = observeN
    sample  = [dup ; +] "double" ; Named

# the plain category: the same functions with nothing attached
def runP     = unPlain ... ; ev
def composeP = (f g -> [f ... ; runP ; g ... ; runP] ; Plain)
def observeP = (f -> f 3 ; runP ; (y -> f 4 ; runP ; (z -> y z ; +)))

model Funcs in Arrow(Plain) =
    embed   = Plain
    compose = composeP
    observe = observeP
    sample  = [dup ; +] ; Plain

# The component: drop the name.  (`forget` is the prelude's word for
# discarding a sum's payload, so this one is `strip`.)
def strip = (c -> c ; witnessN ; Plain)

transformation Forget in Names ⇒ Funcs = strip
#   Forget : Named(ρ, σ) ⇒ Plain(ρ, σ)

def namedSq in Names = sample sample ; compose
namedSq ; unNamed ; drop _ ; print             # doubledouble — the name
namedSq ; Forget ; observeP ; print            # 28 — 3·4 + 4·4

# Both models run the doctrine's five laws at their own samples, and
# the four squares above are checked besides.  `embed` and `sample` are
# PROVED; `compose` and `observe` are SAMPLED, and the reason is the
# 2026-09-16 move itself:
#
#     embed    proved
#     compose  sampled (2 points; ev of an open wire)
#     observe  sampled (1 point; ev of an open wire)
#     sample   proved
#
# Until that date a hom-object pinned its own `Fn⟨a ⇒ b⟩` to ONE WIRE
# on each side, so applying it was a neutral application and the
# normalizer followed all five squares.  Over STACKS the witness is
# `Fn⟨ρ ⇒ σ⟩` with ρ open, and `ev` of an open wire has no closed
# arity — the same wall `Len` has always stood at, reached now by the
# carrier models too.  That is what flat products cost here, said out
# loud: the pairing, the `data Pair` and the routing pass went, and two
# of these four verdicts went with them.  What would buy them back is a
# normalizer that reads a stack's width from the type at the splice,
# which is a stage of its own.
#
# `Len` above proves none and samples all three.  The same declaration,
# and different verdicts, decided rather than chosen.
